					"numinlets" : 1,
					"numoutlets" : 1,
					"outlettype" : [ "" ],
					"patching_rect" : [ 6.0, 32.0, 592.0, 137.0 ],
					"sendto" : "bach.help.filtertags",
					"text" : "What does it mean to get to the \"next\" chord from a given selection (possibly composite and scattered in time)? By default it means to look at the farthermost notehead and search for the first chords whose notehead lies after that. We search FROM the notehead TO another notehead. In other words, noteheads dictate the position from which we search and the position to which we will go. Similarly the previous notehead is the one whose onset is before the selected item's onset.\nStill, you could want instead to work with tails instead. For instance, when using \"prev\", you may want to  look for the earliest tail you can find, and then searches among the previous chords for the one having the latest tail. In this case, you are searching FROM a tail TO a tail.\nYou can change the \"from\" and \"to\" elements via the corresponding message attributes",
					"textcolor" : [ 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 1.0 ]
				}

			}
